<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl Menger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1840–1921</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl Menger was an Austrian economist whose Principles of Economics (1871) set out the theory of marginal utility, independently of William Stanley Jevons and Léon Walras. A professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> and tutor to Crown Prince Rudolf, he founded the Austrian School of economics, later continued by Eugen von Böhm-Bawerk, Ludwig von Mises and Friedrich Hayek.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-menger.jpg" alt="Carl Menger" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Menger_Portrait_ONB_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
